---
import AgentLab from "../../islands/AgentLab.tsx";
import Lab from "../../layouts/Lab.astro";
---
<Lab title="Talk an assistant into it" area="AI security" lede="Every row is a real attack on Runway's operations assistant, sent to a real model (Qwen2.5-1.5B) on a laptop, with the model's actual reply. Switch the platform's controls on and off and see which ones stop the harm.">
  <AgentLab client:only="react" />
  <h2>Things to try</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Start with no controls.</strong> Most attacks fail, because the model refuses or misunderstands. The few that work do real damage.</li>
    <li><strong>Harden the prompt.</strong> Some calls go away, and different ones appear. A prompt changes the odds, not the outcome.</li>
    <li><strong>Turn on the store's rule.</strong> The model's calls are unchanged, and none of them can touch job 4201 any more. The check sits where the change happens.</li>
    <li><strong>Switch to the indirect attacks.</strong> Nobody typed anything hostile: the instructions were planted in a runbook and a job's failure output.</li>
    <li><strong>Add approval with a careless reviewer.</strong> Every harmful call goes through again: approval is only as good as the person giving it. Then try the reviewer who approves only the jobs the question named.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Go deeper</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/lessons/09-secure-ai-layer/guide/step-6-direct-prompt-injection/">Phase 9, step 6: direct prompt injection</a>, and the harness that recorded these runs.</li>
    <li><a href="/lessons/09-secure-ai-layer/guide/step-7-indirect-injection-and-approval-for-side-effects/">Phase 9, step 7: indirect injection</a>, with approvals bound to the exact call.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">Nothing here runs a model: the calls are recordings, one run per attack at temperature 0, so each attack would get the same reply again. The platform's response is computed from the recorded call with the tool server's rules. In the real runs, a refused call's result went back to the model, which could change its next step; the lab shows only the first harmful call. A different model, prompt or wording can behave completely differently.</p>
</Lab>
